# Fixtures and isolation

Every test gets its own `page` and `BrowserContext`. Do not reuse a page, storage, or mutable helper across tests.

## Prefer fixtures over ad-hoc setup

When the same page object is constructed in several specs, extend `test` instead of newing it in every case.

```typescript
import { test as base } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoginPage } from '../pages/LoginPage';

export const test = base.extend<{ loginPage: LoginPage }>({
  loginPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);
    await use(loginPage);
  },
});

export { expect } from '@playwright/test';
```

Put shared fixtures under `fixtures/` and import them from specs. Keep `test` from `@playwright/test` only when no custom fixture is needed.

## Isolation rules

- Do not store data on `page` or module-level variables between tests.
- Auth belongs in `storageState` / project dependencies when you add it, not in copied login steps everywhere.
- Clean up in the fixture after `use` if the fixture created extra context or test data.
- Specs assert outcomes. Fixtures and page objects set up and act; they do not own business assertions.
